1
00:00:02,390 --> 00:00:03,460
Who is it?

2
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:05,330
Dad.

3
00:00:05,330 --> 00:00:09,740
Come on in, Dad.

4
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:11,840
I thought you might be hungry.

5
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:14,530
I brought you a chicken sandwich and a glass of milk.

6
00:00:14,530 --> 00:00:15,570
I am hungry.

7
00:00:15,570 --> 00:00:18,510
Thanks, Dad.

8
00:00:18,510 --> 00:00:22,550
What time is it, anyway?

9
00:00:22,550 --> 00:00:25,890
Ten o'clock.

10
00:00:25,890 --> 00:00:27,430
What are you working on?

11
00:00:27,430 --> 00:00:31,040
I'm writing a story for the high-school paper.

12
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:32,490
Can't you finish it tomorrow?

13
00:00:32,490 --> 00:00:36,520
No, I have to turn it in in the morning.

14
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:39,240
What's it about?

15
00:00:39,240 --> 00:00:43,530
I'm writing an article on the feelings about graduation.

16
00:00:43,530 --> 00:00:46,490
And ... how do you feel?

17
00:00:46,490 --> 00:00:48,930
Me?

18
00:00:48,930 --> 00:00:50,610
A little scared.

19
00:00:50,610 --> 00:00:54,040
And excited, too.

20
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:55,750
I felt the same way.

21
00:00:55,750 --> 00:00:58,930
The scary part's leaving home and moving to college.

22
00:00:58,930 --> 00:01:03,950
Oh, leaving home is part of growing up.

23
00:01:03,950 --> 00:01:07,180
Well, don't work all night.

24
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:08,020
I don't mind.

25
00:01:08,020 --> 00:01:09,920
I enjoy writing.

26
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:10,610
Well,

27
00:01:10,610 --> 00:01:12,530
maybe you should think about becoming a writer.

28
00:01:12,530 --> 00:01:14,780
Maybe I should.

29
00:01:14,780 --> 00:01:17,330
You have lots of time to decide.

30
00:01:17,330 --> 00:01:21,270
That's the worst part--making decisions.

31
00:01:21,270 --> 00:01:22,960
You'll be OK.

32
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:24,250
Good night, Son.

33
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:35,260
Good night, Dad.

34
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:37,400
"Hail to the victors valiant,

35
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:39,580
Hail to the conquering heroes,

36
00:01:39,580 --> 00:01:41,990
Hail, Hail to Michigan,

37
00:01:41,990 --> 00:01:44,070
The champions of the West!"

38
00:01:44,070 --> 00:01:47,570
Ah, good morning, Robbie.

39
00:01:47,570 --> 00:01:48,740
Good morning, Dad.

40
00:01:48,740 --> 00:01:50,220
How's my grandson?

41
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:52,280
Fine, Grandpa. Fine!

42
00:01:52,280 --> 00:01:54,030
What's all the cheering about?

43
00:01:54,030 --> 00:01:56,840
Did the University of Michigan win another football game?

44
00:01:56,840 --> 00:01:58,470
Better than that.

45
00:01:58,470 --> 00:01:59,720
Tell him, Philip.

46
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:02,160
I just spoke with Charley Rafer.

47
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:04,180
Who's Charley Rafer?

48
00:02:04,180 --> 00:02:06,940
He's the Dean of Admissions for the University of Michigan.

49
00:02:06,940 --> 00:02:09,780
And it turns out he's a classmate of Philip's.

50
00:02:09,780 --> 00:02:12,130
We were both on the tennis team.

51
00:02:12,130 --> 00:02:13,940
Great!

52
00:02:13,940 --> 00:02:15,340
It is great.

53
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:16,830
He's going to be in New York tomorrow

54
00:02:16,830 --> 00:02:19,270
to interview applicants for admission.

55
00:02:19,270 --> 00:02:22,800
And he's agreed to fit you into his schedule.

56
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:25,590
But I may not want to go to Michigan.

57
00:02:25,590 --> 00:02:28,490
It's one of the best schools in the country, Robbie.

58
00:02:28,490 --> 00:02:29,780
I studied medicine there.

59
00:02:29,780 --> 00:02:32,820
Your grandfather went to the Engineering School there.

60
00:02:32,820 --> 00:02:34,500
I know that, but ...

61
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:37,260
You said you wanted to be a doctor like your father.

62
00:02:37,260 --> 00:02:39,100
Not exactly.

63
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:42,190
You couldn't pick a finer medical school than Michigan.

64
00:02:42,190 --> 00:02:44,060
Yes, I know that.

65
00:02:44,060 --> 00:02:47,260
Let's meet with Charley at the university club.

66
00:02:47,260 --> 00:02:49,590
Ten o'clock tomorrow morning.

67
00:02:49,590 --> 00:02:52,700
It doesn't mean you're going to Michigan.

68
00:02:52,700 --> 00:02:54,930
It doesn't mean you have to be a doctor.

69
00:02:54,930 --> 00:02:58,420
But the interview will be good experience for you.

70
00:02:58,420 --> 00:03:01,980
In that case, it's OK.

71
00:03:01,980 --> 00:03:04,530
Dad, growing up means

72
00:03:04,530 --> 00:03:07,930
making my own decisions, doesn't it?

73
00:03:07,930 --> 00:03:09,360
You're right, Robbie.

74
00:03:09,360 --> 00:03:13,410
But, like your Grandpa suggested,

75
00:03:13,410 --> 00:03:15,720
have the interview.

76
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:19,820
And then make your own decision.

77
00:03:19,820 --> 00:03:21,520
That sounds fine.

78
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:22,720
I know it's sudden, Robbie,

79
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:26,350
but this is an important opportunity.

80
00:03:26,350 --> 00:03:28,190
We'll head down there first thing tomorrow morning.

81
00:03:28,190 --> 00:03:28,880
OK?

82
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:33,160
Sure, Dad.

83
00:03:33,160 --> 00:03:37,350
I want you to know something, Son.

84
00:03:37,350 --> 00:03:41,470
I'm ... very proud of you.

85
00:03:41,470 --> 00:03:45,090
Thanks, Dad.

86
00:03:45,090 --> 00:03:47,340
Well, I've got an appointment at the hospital.

87
00:03:47,340 --> 00:03:49,710
I'll see you all at dinnertime.

88
00:03:49,710 --> 00:03:52,920
Bye, Dad.

89
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:58,420
Is something still wrong, Robbie?

90
00:03:58,420 --> 00:04:01,030
I'll be OK.

91
00:04:01,030 --> 00:04:04,020
Going away to college for the first time

92
00:04:04,020 --> 00:04:08,950
always makes one a little nervous.

93
00:04:08,950 --> 00:04:10,760
I guess so.

94
00:04:10,760 --> 00:04:13,760
I'll be OK. I just need time to think.
